  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute uncomplicated cystitis (N30.00)</content>. Typical lower urinary symptoms with a positive dipstick and no fever, flank pain, or systemic signs, so pyelonephritis is not suspected. eGFR 73 at intake allows nitrofurantoin. [S]<list><item>Nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice a day with food for 5 days.</item><item>Dosing instructions and warning signs given in braille, and the bottle marked with a braille label.</item><item>Call or return for fever, flank pain, vomiting, or no improvement in 48 hours; a urine culture would then be sent.</item><item>Usual fluids; no need to delay the trip if symptoms have cleared.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Fall risk (Z91.81)</content>. Night-time trips to the bathroom raise the fall risk while symptoms last. [S]<list><item>Keep the bedside route clear and use the rail her occupational therapist advised.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: STEADI (Stopping Elderly Accidents, Deaths and Injuries): Clinical Resources (2024); Zhou SY, et al. Training Medical Students in Deaf, DeafBlind, and Hard-of-Hearing Patient Care Through Interactive Lecture and Simulation. MedEdPORTAL 2025;21:11566 (2025); International Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Treatment of Acute Uncomplicated Cystitis and Pyelonephritis in Women: A 2010 Update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America and the European Society for Microbiology and Infectious Diseases (2011)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse check by text relay in two days. Falls review as planned in September. Next scheduled visit 1959-09-14: six-month falls prevention and function review. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
